{
  "markdown": "In Pydantic v2.8.2, validators are skipped when using default values unless `validate_default=True` is set in the field definition [[cite:ref_0002]]. This behavior mirrors Pydantic v1's `always=True` parameter on the `validator` decorator, which forced validation on defaults [[cite:ref_0002]]. The supplied evidence does not explicitly confirm the default behavior of validators in Pydantic v1.10.17 without this flag, only noting that `always=True` was the mechanism to enable it [[cite:ref_0002]].",
  "title": "Pydantic Validator Behavior on Default Values",
  "summary": "Pydantic v2.8.2 skips validators for default values by default, requiring `validate_default=True` to enforce them, a behavior analogous to v1's `always=True` flag. Evidence is insufficient to confirm whether v1.10.17 validates defaults without explicit configuration.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "heading": "V2 Default Skipping",
      "narrative": "Validators do not run when a field's default value is used; they must be explicitly enabled via `Field(validate_default=True)` [[cite:ref_0002]].",
      "evidence_ids": [
       "ref_0002"
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "V2 Override Mechanism",
      "narrative": "The `validate_default=True` argument in `Field()` forces validation on default values, as demonstrated in code examples where annotated fields are validated while others are not [[cite:ref_0001]][[cite:ref_0002]].",
      "evidence_ids": [
       "ref_0001",
       "ref_0002"
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "V1 Comparison",
      "narrative": "Setting `validate_default=True` in v2 provides behavior closest to using `always=True` in v1's `validator` decorator, implying v1 required this flag to validate defaults [[cite:ref_0002]].",
      "evidence_ids": [
       "ref_0002"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Evidence does not explicitly confirm if Pydantic v1.10.17 validators run on defaults without `always=True`."
  ]
}

